Long formulas were the one thing this blog handled badly. KaTeX renders them beautifully, but a
matrix product that is wider than 68 characters used to push the whole article sideways, and
there was nowhere to say what the symbols meant except in the paragraph after it.

So display math now gets its own figure — a plate with a scroll box, a maximise button and an
optional note. Nothing about the way I write it changed.

## The syntax

A formula on one line, with the note after the closing `$$`:

```markdown
$$a^2 + b^2 = c^2$$ Pythagoras' theorem.
```

$$a^2 + b^2 = c^2$$ Pythagoras' theorem.

The note is optional. Left off, the plate is just the formula:

$$e^{i\pi} + 1 = 0$$

It is ordinary markdown in there, so *emphasis*, [links](https://katex.org/docs/supported) and
the blog's own citation syntax all work:

$$H(X) = -\sum_{x \in \mathcal{X}} p(x) \log_2 p(x)$$ Shannon entropy, in bits — the average
surprise of a draw from $X$. See [@chen2005, p. 3] for the estimator I keep coming back to.

## Formulas with more than one line

The fenced form takes a note too. It goes on the line straight after the closing fence — a blank
line there means you wanted a new paragraph, and you get one:

```markdown
$$
E = m c^2
$$
The note, on the line straight after the closing fence.
```

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathcal{L}(\theta) &= -\frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^{N} \log p_\theta(y_i \mid x_i) \\
\nabla_\theta \mathcal{L}(\theta) &= -\frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^{N} \nabla_\theta \log p_\theta(y_i \mid x_i) \\
&= -\frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^{N} \frac{\nabla_\theta\, p_\theta(y_i \mid x_i)}{p_\theta(y_i \mid x_i)}
\end{aligned}
$$
The gradient of the negative log-likelihood, written out once so I never have to derive it again.

Inline math is untouched by all of this: $\nabla_\theta$ in a sentence is still a symbol in a
sentence, not a figure.

## Wide, and dense

This is where the plate earns its keep. The formula below is far wider than the column, so it
scrolls inside its own box — drag it, or shift-scroll over it — while the page itself stays put:

$$
\underbrace{\Big[ \sum_{k=1}^{n} \alpha_k \, \phi_k(x) \Big]}_{\text{basis expansion}} \cdot \underbrace{\Big[ \prod_{j=1}^{m} \big(1 - \gamma_j e^{-\lambda_j t}\big) \Big]}_{\text{decay}} + \underbrace{\int_{0}^{T} \! \kappa(t, s) \, u(s) \, \mathrm{d}s}_{\text{memory term}} == \underbrace{\varepsilon \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2)}_{\text{what is left}}= \underbrace{\varepsilon \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2)}_{\text{what is left}}= \underbrace{\varepsilon \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2)}_{\text{what is left}} \underbrace{\varepsilon \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2)}_{\text{what is left}}
$$
Nothing here means anything. It is wide on purpose.

And when scrolling a strip of it is not enough, the button in the top right corner lifts the
plate out of the article and over the page, at a size that scales with the viewport. Escape
closes it again, or a click anywhere outside.

## How it works

`$$…$$` never reaches the browser as LaTeX. A remark plugin in `svelte.config.js` finds the
display math, hands the formula to KaTeX at build time and wraps the result in a small Svelte
figure, which is also where the note ends up as a `figcaption`. The import for that figure is
injected into the post automatically, so a post stays a plain markdown file — one that my
Obsidian vault, where all of these are written, renders exactly the same way.

What ships to you is static HTML plus KaTeX's stylesheet. The only JavaScript on the page is the
maximise toggle.
